Q5. What are the four pillars of OOP? Explain each with examples.
========================

ANSWER:

The four pillars of Object-Oriented Programming are:

1. Encapsulation
2. Inheritance
3. Abstraction
4. Polymorphism

Encapsulation means bundling data and methods into a single unit and controlling access to the data, usually using private fields and methods such as getters and setters.

Inheritance allows a child class to acquire properties and behavior from a parent class. For example, Car and Bike can inherit common properties from Vehicle.

Abstraction means hiding implementation details and exposing only the required functionality. For example, an ATM allows us to withdraw money without exposing its internal implementation.

Polymorphism means one interface or method name can have multiple forms. It can be achieved through method overloading at compile time and method overriding at runtime.


