Q9. What is the difference between == and .equals() in Java?
========================

ANSWER:

For objects, == compares whether two references point to the same object, whereas .equals() is generally used to compare logical content, depending on how the class implements it.

For example, two String objects can contain the same text but be different objects.

In that case, == may return false while .equals() returns true.


