Q27. What is the difference between a process and a thread?
========================

ANSWER:

A process is an independent program in execution with its own memory space and resources.

A thread is the smallest unit of execution within a process.

Multiple threads of the same process share resources such as the heap and code section, but each thread has its own stack and execution state.

Processes are generally more heavyweight, while threads are comparatively lightweight.


